":"Scored on the CURRENT-YEAR suite: the Treasury Management Strategy for 2026/27 (cover report and Appendix A both to Cabinet on 3 February 2026, recommended to Council; the appendix is a tracked-change refresh of the version Council approved on 25 February 2025, headed 'FEBRUARY 2025 2026' with indicators explicitly reset for 2026/2027) together with the Capital Strategy 2026/27 (Cabinet 3 February 2026, Appendix A), which is where prudential indicators 1-5 live because TMS s11.2-11.3 p.13 delegates them to it. The 2026/27 TMS Appendix A and cover report are authoritative human-supplied copies (the committee site is behind a Cloudflare challenge); the Capital Strategy, HRA capital programme and MTFS forecast appendices were fetched from the council's committee site. Strongest areas: the borrowing strategy and its quantified internal-borrowing position (GBP 131-136m against CFR), the creditworthiness and counterparty framework (CDS and non-ratings information, minimum Moody's A3/P-2 criteria, per-counterparty, group, sector and country limits at Annexes F-H), a complete four-balance liability benchmark to 2029, commercial-investment governance (IST/IGB, independent Carter Jonas review, proportionality and dependence indicators) and a full reporting cycle - annual strategy to full Council, quarterly performance reports and an annual outturn to the Audit and Corporate Governance Committee. Weakest areas: the strategy cross-refers at s8.10 p.7 to fixed/variable interest-rate exposure limits that its own indicator set (s12, pp.14-15, checked visually) does not contain; nothing is split General Fund / HRA - one combined liability benchmark, an unsplit CFR and a General-Fund-only financing-costs ratio - despite GBP 205.123m of HRA self-financing PWLB debt plus c.GBP 129m of HRA new-build loans; and the non-treasury Investment Strategy underpinning most commercial disclosures has not been refreshed since 30 March 2023, so its indicator series ends at 2025/26 and does not cover the year being scored (the TMS still carries the unchanged line at s1.6 p.2 that 'it is proposed to update the strategy early in 2025/2026'). Also absent: any investment management practices (IMPs), a sterling-only borrowing statement, and any flag for audit qualification of source data.","gaps":[{"id":"PI-07","evidence":"Capital Strategy 2026/27 (Cabinet 3 Feb 2026, App A) Annex A indicator 5 p.15: financing costs GBP 4,730k (2024/25 actual) to 5,613k (2028/29) and 15.1%/11.7%/14.7%/17.8%/22.3% of net revenue stream"},{"id":"LB-03","evidence":"TMS 2026/27 (Cabinet 3 Feb 2026, App A) s12.1E p.15: benchmark described as 'an important tool to help establish whether the Council is likely to be a long-term borrower or long-term investor in the future, and so shape its strategic focus'; internal borrowing of GBP 131.2m-135.6m explained at s6.2 p.5 and Annex C p.25"},{"id":"LB-04","evidence":""},{"id":"COM-02","evidence":"Capital Strategy 2026/27 (Cabinet 3 Feb 2026, App A) s12.1 p.12 'This Strategy covers the requirements of the updated 2021 Prudential Code, including the Prudential Indicators'; Investment Strategy 2023 s4.3 p.7 'The Council has noted and has had regard to the Guidance and has no plans to borrow in advance of need and is, therefore, compliant with the CIPFA Prudential Code in respect of this matter'"},{"id":"COM-05","evidence":""},{"id":"BOR-03","evidence":""}],"documents":[{"title":"Treasury Management Strategy 2026/27 - Appendix A (Cabinet 3 February 2026 item 9, recommended to Council) [supplied copy: monitor/compliance/source_pdfs/E07000012/s142131-app-a.pdf]","url":"https://scambs.moderngov.co.uk/documents/s142131/App%20A.pdf","fy":"2026/27"},{"title":"Treasury Management Strategy - cover report to Cabinet 3 February 2026 [supplied copy: monitor/compliance/source_pdfs/E07000012/s142104-treasury-management-strategy.pdf]","url":"https://scambs.moderngov.co.uk/documents/s142104/Treasury%20Management%20Strategy.pdf","fy":"2026/27"},{"title":"Capit